use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Traversal:
    description:
        Traversal lets the user move between the items of a list, grid, or
        group with the keyboard, without leaving it.

    movement:
        In something that runs one way, like a list, the arrow keys in that
        direction move to the previous and next item. Home and End go to the
        first and last item. In a list that scrolls, Page Up and Page Down move
        about one screenful.

        In something that runs both ways, like a calendar or a grid of colour
        swatches, Left and Right move along a row and Up and Down move between
        rows. Home and End go to the start and end of the row. Anything beyond
        that follows the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices grid pattern.

    direction:
        Left and Right match what the user sees. Where a right-to-left language
        flips the layout, Left moves to the next item and Right to the
        previous one. Up and Down are not affected.

    tabStop:
        Pressing Tab moves into the whole group in one step, and pressing it
        again moves out. Tabbing in lands on the item the user was last on, so
        they pick up where they left off. A few components give every item its
        own Tab stop as well as arrow keys, such as the headings of an
        Accordion; they say so themselves.

    bounds:
        Whether the arrow keys stop at the first and last item, or wrap around
        to the other end, follows the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices for that kind
        of component. Radio groups and tabs wrap. Lists, trees and grids stop,
        so the user can tell they have reached the end. Menus and toolbars can
        do either, and the component decides.

    disabledItems:
        Whether the arrow keys land on a disabled item or skip over it also
        follows the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices. Menus and lists land on
        disabled items, so screen reader users know the option exists. Radio
        groups skip them. Either way, a disabled item can't be used.

    typeAhead:
        Typing a letter jumps to the next item whose label starts with it.
        Typing several letters quickly matches the whole word typed. Nothing
        is hidden, which is what makes this different from Filtering.
        Components that already have a search box leave typing to that box.
